---
// Make screens with AI. Opens with the one prompt to paste to an agent (firmware/ai-prompt.txt: the same file the
// panel serves at GET /ai with its own address in; the address field here puts yours where it says {panel}), then
// what a screen spec is, why AI-made screens still look like Facet (components + tokens, budgets, validation on the
// panel), the panel's API (API keys: asked for by name, allowed on the glass; core/keys.h), Home Assistant, examples.
// Examples: a grid of tiles (WASM shots, dark + light, follow the site theme: renderer/tools/readme_assets.mjs --ai-examples) linking to ./examples/<id>/.
import Docs, { type Heading } from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { AI_EXAMPLES, url } from '../../lib/nav';
import examples from '../../lib/ai-examples.json';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import promptText from '../../../../firmware/ai-prompt.txt?raw';

const headings: Heading[] = [
  { depth: 2, slug: 'prompt', text: 'Paste this to your agent' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'specs', text: 'What the agent makes' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'panel', text: 'The panel\'s API' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'home-assistant', text: 'Through Home Assistant' },
  ...(AI_EXAMPLES ? [{ depth: 2, slug: 'examples', text: 'Examples' }] : []),
];
const ADDR = 'http://<panel address>';
const prompt = promptText.replaceAll('{panel}', ADDR);
const curl = `P=http://<panel address>
curl -s -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"name":"My script on my laptop"}' $P/fw/keys/request  # tap Allow on the panel
curl -s -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"id":"<id>"}' $P/fw/keys/poll   # the key, once
T=<key>
curl -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $T" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"kind":"check","spec":'"$(cat bin_day.json)"'}' $P/fw/spec    # valid, or what to fix
curl -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $T" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"kind":"screen","spec":'"$(cat bin_day.json)"'}' $P/fw/spec   # install it
curl -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $T" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"screen":"bin_day"}' $P/fw/show                               # open it
curl -s -o shot.bmp $P/screenshot                     # see what the glass shows`;
const ha = `# install (answers like POST /fw/spec: applied, or the errors to fix)
action: esphome.kitchen_panel_facet_screen
data:
  spec: >-
    {"v": 1, "id": "bin_day", "name": "Bin day", ... }
response_variable: result

# open it
action: esphome.kitchen_panel_facet_show
data:
  screen: bin_day`;
---

<Docs edit="site/src/pages/ai/index.astro" title="Make screens with AI" description="Ask an AI assistant for any screen you like: it writes a Facet spec, the panel checks it, and it looks like the rest of Facet." group="Inside Facet" current={url('/ai/')} headings={headings}>
  <p class="lede">Describe a screen, for example your bin days or the next trains, and an AI assistant writes it as a spec. The panel checks the spec, and the screen uses the same components and tokens as the built-in ones.</p>

  <h2 id="prompt">Paste this to your agent</h2>
  <p>Type your panel's address, copy the prompt, add what you want at the end and paste it to your agent. Your panel serves the same prompt at <code>http://&lt;panel address&gt;/ai</code>, its address already in.</p>
  <label class="ai-addr">Panel address <input type="text" inputmode="url" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" placeholder="192.168.1.40" data-ai-addr /></label>
  <div data-ai-prompt data-addr={ADDR}>
    <CodeBlock id="agent-prompt" code={prompt} label="Prompt" copyLabel="Copy prompt" />
  </div>
  <p>The agent asks the panel for an API key with its name, the same way any script or automation does, and the panel asks you <em>Allow &lt;name&gt;?</em> on its screen. Tap <strong>Allow</strong>. Every change it makes shows on the panel with <strong>Undo</strong>; <strong>Settings &gt; API keys</strong> lists the keys and revokes them. Keys need <strong>Allow API access</strong> on, in the panel's Settings. It is plain HTTP on your own Wi-Fi: the panel's web page is open on the LAN, as panels at this tier are.</p>

  <h2 id="specs">What the agent makes</h2>
  <p>
    The assistant writes the screen as a <a href={url('/inside/spec/')}>spec</a>: a short JSON list of Facet components.
    The panel checks every spec against its own budgets and components before it keeps it and says what to fix, so a
    screen made this way looks like the rest of Facet. <a href={url('/inside/spec/')}>Spec format</a> explains the format
    and its limits.
  </p>

  <h2 id="panel">The panel's API</h2>
  <p>For agents, scripts and automations that make web requests: each asks for its own API key the same way. The panel serves its own guide at <code>/llms.txt</code> and <code>/agents.md</code>, generated from the firmware itself: every component and prop it accepts, how to bind entities, the actions and the budgets. Every request it answers is listed on <a href={url('/inside/schema/#routes')}>Schema</a>.</p>
  <p>Every write needs <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;key&gt;</code>; reads need nothing. Without the setting on the panel answers 403, without a working key 401, each with one line saying what to do. A key ends after 30 days unused (each use moves its end on), after 1 day whatever happens, or when revoked, as chosen when it is asked for; a key kept until revoked still goes when it is not used for 90 days.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={curl} lang="bash" label="curl" copyLabel="Copy curl commands" />

  <h2 id="home-assistant">Through Home Assistant</h2>
  <p>Assistants that already work with your Home Assistant can install screens through the panel's ESPHome actions, with no network access to the panel: <code>esphome.&lt;panel&gt;_facet_hello</code> (components, screens and budgets), <code>_facet_screen</code> (install a screen spec), <code>_facet_deck</code> (the page list), <code>_facet_revert</code> (back to the factory screen) and <code>_facet_show</code> (open a screen). The first four answer with a response, so call them with a response variable and read the errors.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={ha} lang="yaml" label="Home Assistant · YAML" copyLabel="Copy Home Assistant actions" />

  {AI_EXAMPLES && (<>
  <h2 id="examples">Examples</h2>
  <p>Screens made only from the released components, each using its components the way their own demos do: lists, charts, tiles, checklists, stats and a slider. Open one to try it live, copy its spec or copy the prompt that makes it. Sample data: made-up devices, not a real home.</p>
  <ul class="ex-grid">
    {examples.map((e) => (
      <li>
        <a href={url(`/ai/examples/${e.id}/`)}>
          <img class="shot shot-dark" src={url(`/ai/shots/${e.id}-dark.png`)} alt="" width="360" height="360" loading="lazy" />
          <img class="shot shot-light" src={url(`/ai/shots/${e.id}-light.png`)} alt="" width="360" height="360" loading="lazy" />
          <strong>{e.title}</strong>
          <span>{e.about}</span>
        </a>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>
  </>)}
</Docs>

<script>
  // the address field: the prompt's address (every place it says it) follows what is typed
  const input = document.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('[data-ai-addr]');
  const box = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-ai-prompt]');
  if (input && box) {
    let cur = box.dataset.addr ?? '';
    input.addEventListener('input', () => {
      const v = input.value.trim().replace(/\/+$/, '');
      const next = v ? (/^https?:\/\//.test(v) ? v : `http://${v}`) : (box.dataset.addr ?? '');
      if (!cur || next === cur) return;
      const walk = document.createTreeWalker(box.querySelector('pre') ?? box, NodeFilter.SHOW_TEXT);
      for (let n = walk.nextNode(); n; n = walk.nextNode()) if (n.nodeValue?.includes(cur)) n.nodeValue = n.nodeValue.split(cur).join(next);
      cur = next;
    });
  }
</script>

<style>
  .ai-addr { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; gap: 8px 12px; margin: 16px 0; color: var(--facet-color-text-dim); font-size: 14px; }
  .ai-addr input { flex: 1 1 220px; max-width: 360px; padding: 8px 12px; border: 1px solid var(--facet-color-line-hi); border-radius: 10px; background: var(--facet-color-surface); color: var(--facet-color-text); font: inherit; font-size: 16px; }
  .ai-addr input:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--facet-color-accent); outline-offset: 2px; }
  .ex-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(200px, 1fr)); gap: 16px; padding: 0; list-style: none; }
  .ex-grid a { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 4px; height: 100%; padding: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--facet-color-line); border-radius: 16px; background: var(--facet-color-surface); color: var(--facet-color-text); text-decoration: none; }
  .ex-grid a:hover { border-color: var(--facet-color-line-hi); background: var(--facet-color-surface-press); }
  .ex-grid .shot { width: 100%; height: auto; margin-bottom: 8px; border-radius: 12px; }
  .ex-grid .shot-light { display: none; }
  :global(:root[data-theme='light']) .ex-grid .shot-light { display: block; }
  :global(:root[data-theme='light']) .ex-grid .shot-dark { display: none; }
  .ex-grid span { color: var(--facet-color-text-dim); font-size: 14px; line-height: 20px; }
</style>

